        <div id="short-version" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Version: Four Mechanisms, Many Shapes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every humidifier on sale puts water into the air in one of four ways: it evaporates water off a wet wick with a fan, it shakes water into a fine mist with an ultrasonic plate, it flings water against a diffuser with a spinning disc, or it boils water into steam. Everything else on the shelf, from a palm-sized desk unit to a furnace-mounted system, is one of those four mechanisms in a different body. Once you know which mechanism you are looking at, most of the marketing language stops mattering.
          </p>

          <p>
            The mechanism decides the things that actually affect daily life: whether you get white dust on the furniture, how loud the unit is at night, how much electricity it draws, what you have to replace, and how much cleaning it demands. The body, or form factor, decides how much water it holds, how large a space it can realistically serve, and where it can go. Shoppers tend to choose by form factor first, which is why so many people end up with a quiet ultrasonic unit that coats a black television stand in mineral dust.
          </p>

          <p>
            This guide maps every common type, says what each one is good at, and points to the deeper comparisons where a single choice deserves a full page. If you already know you want a specific type and just want a recommendation, our <a href="/best-humidifier/">best humidifier picks</a> are organised by type. If you are still deciding, read the sections below in order; the decision table at the end pulls it all together.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">How it adds moisture</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">White dust</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical power</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Evaporative</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fan blows air through a wet wick</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Ultrasonic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Vibrating plate makes a cool mist</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, with tap water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Impeller</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Spinning disc flings water at a diffuser</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, with tap water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Steam / warm mist</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Heating element boils water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No (scale stays inside)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hybrid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ultrasonic mist from pre-warmed water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, with tap water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low to high</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            One rule applies to every type: a humidifier only helps when it holds the room in a sensible range. The EPA recommends keeping indoor relative humidity between 30 and 50 percent, and above roughly 60 percent you start inviting condensation, mold and dust mites. Our guide to <a href="/ideal-indoor-humidity/">ideal indoor humidity</a> covers where to set it as the weather changes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="evaporative" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Evaporative (Wick) Humidifiers
          </h2>

          <p>
            An evaporative humidifier is the simplest design. Water from the tank soaks into a wick or filter made of paper, foam or a similar absorbent material, and a fan pulls room air through it. The air picks up moisture as it passes, exactly the way a wet towel dries on a line. There is no visible mist, just slightly cooler, more humid air coming out of the top or front of the unit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Its best trait is that it regulates itself. Evaporation slows down as the air around it gets more humid, so an evaporative unit is far less likely to push a room past 60 percent than a mist-maker running flat out. The second big advantage is cleanliness of the output: dissolved minerals in tap water stay behind in the wick instead of being sprayed into the room, so there is no white dust. These are the units most commonly sold as humidifiers with filters, because the wick is the filter.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trade-offs are noise and consumables. Because the fan is doing all the work, an evaporative humidifier makes a steady whooshing sound, which some people like as white noise and others cannot sleep through. The wick hardens with minerals over time and needs replacing; manufacturers typically suggest every one to three months during the heating season, and hard water shortens that. Factor the cost of wicks into the price before you buy, because it adds up over a winter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Evaporative humidifiers suit living rooms, open-plan spaces and anyone on hard water who does not want to buy distilled water. Large console evaporative units are the default choice for big rooms, which is why they dominate our <a href="/best-humidifier-for-large-room/">large room humidifier picks</a>. For a head-to-head on the most common choice shoppers face, see <a href="/evaporative-vs-ultrasonic-humidifier/">evaporative vs ultrasonic humidifiers</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ultrasonic" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Ultrasonic and Impeller: The Cool Mist Sprayers
          </h2>

          <p>
            An ultrasonic humidifier has a small ceramic or metal plate, called a transducer, sitting under a shallow pool of water. It vibrates at a frequency above human hearing, which breaks the surface of the water into droplets small enough to drift out of the nozzle as a visible cool mist. There is no heater and usually only a small fan, so ultrasonic units are typically the quietest humidifiers you can buy and among the cheapest to run.
          </p>

          <p>
            The weakness is that the mist is liquid water, and whatever is dissolved in that water leaves with it. When the droplets evaporate, minerals from tap water settle as fine white dust on surfaces nearby. The EPA has noted that ultrasonic and impeller humidifiers can disperse minerals and microorganisms from the tank into the air, and recommends low-mineral water. In practice that means distilled or demineralized water, or a demineralization cartridge, if your tap water is hard.
          </p>

          <p>
            Impeller humidifiers produce a similar cool mist mechanically: a spinning disc throws water against a comb-like diffuser that breaks it into droplets. They share the ultrasonic unit's white dust and dispersal issue, tend to be a little louder, and are now mostly found in inexpensive or novelty children's models. If a product says cool mist and does not mention a wick, it is almost always ultrasonic or impeller.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many ultrasonic models are filterless, which shoppers often read as low maintenance. It means no wicks to buy, but the tank and transducer still need a regular descale and disinfecting, because standing water is exactly what bacteria and mold want. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-clean-a-humidifier/">cleaning a humidifier</a> covers the weekly routine. Ultrasonic units are popular for bedrooms precisely because they are quiet; our <a href="/best-humidifier-for-bedroom/">bedroom humidifier picks</a> lean on that.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE WATER RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Your tap water decides whether a mist humidifier suits you.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">On soft water, an ultrasonic humidifier is quiet, cheap to run and nearly maintenance-free. On hard water, it will leave white dust unless you buy distilled water or cartridges. If you are not willing to do either, choose an evaporative or steam type instead.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="warm-mist" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Steam, Warm Mist and Hybrid Units
          </h2>

          <p>
            A steam humidifier, often sold as a warm mist humidifier or vaporizer, heats water to boiling and releases the vapor, which cools to a gentle warm mist by the time it leaves the unit. Boiling has two real advantages: it kills most microbes in the water, and the minerals stay behind as scale on the heating element rather than ending up on your furniture. That makes steam the cleanest output of any portable type.
          </p>

          <p>
            The costs are electricity and safety. A heating element draws far more power than a fan or transducer; warm mist units are commonly rated somewhere around 200 to 400 watts against tens of watts for ultrasonic or evaporative models. The hot water inside is also a scald risk, which is why pediatric guidance commonly recommends cool mist rather than warm mist humidifiers in children's rooms. The mist does not noticeably warm a room, despite the name.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hybrid humidifiers combine an ultrasonic transducer with a heater that warms the water before it is misted. You get a choice of cool or warm mist from one machine, and some models claim the heating step reduces microbes. But the output is still ultrasonic mist, so tap-water minerals still become white dust, and warm mode raises power draw. Treat a hybrid as an ultrasonic unit with an option, not as a steam humidifier.
          </p>

          <p>
            Steam units suit people who want the cleanest possible output, who use the mist alongside a medicated inhalant cup, or who simply prefer warm air in winter, as long as there are no small children or pets who could knock one over. Our <a href="/best-warm-mist-humidifier/">best warm mist humidifier</a> guide covers specific models, and <a href="/warm-mist-vs-cool-mist-humidifier/">warm mist vs cool mist</a> works through the safety and running-cost trade-off in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="form-factors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Console, Tabletop and Personal: Sizes, Not Mechanisms
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once you have chosen a mechanism, the form factor decides capacity. Personal and tabletop humidifiers are the smallest: often under a liter for desk or travel units, and a few liters for bedside models. They are meant for the air immediately around you rather than a whole room, which is fine at a desk or next to a bed but disappointing if you expect one to fix a dry living room.
          </p>

          <p>
            Standard room humidifiers, the most common category, typically hold somewhere between one and two gallons and are rated by manufacturers for a single bedroom or living area. This is where most ultrasonic, evaporative and warm mist models sit. Pay more attention to the rated output, usually given in gallons per day or milliliters per hour, than to the coverage figure on the box, which assumes ideal conditions. Our guide to <a href="/what-size-humidifier-do-i-need/">what size humidifier you need</a> explains the math.
          </p>

          <p>
            Console humidifiers are large floor-standing units, nearly always evaporative, with tanks commonly in the three to six gallon range and manufacturer coverage ratings that run into whole floors of a house. They are the practical answer for open plans and for homes without forced-air ducts, where a whole-house system is not an option. The downsides are size, the weight of a full tank when you refill it, and wick replacement on a larger scale.
          </p>

          <p>
            Materials are a smaller decision but one that searchers ask about. Most humidifiers use plastic tanks, while some premium models use a <a href="/stainless-steel-humidifier/">stainless steel humidifier</a> tank or reservoir, which is easier to scrub and does not pick up odors the way scratched plastic can. Smart features, such as app control and a built-in humidistat that switches the unit on and off to hold a set level, are available across every form factor and are worth having if you would otherwise forget to turn the unit down.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="whole-house" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Whole-House Humidifiers: Bypass, Fan-Powered and Steam
          </h2>

          <p>
            A whole-house or central humidifier is mounted on the ductwork of a forced-air furnace and humidifies the air the heating system already moves through the home. It connects to a water line and, for most types, a drain, and it is controlled by a humidistat, ideally one with an outdoor temperature sensor that lowers the setpoint as it gets colder outside. There is no tank to refill, which is the main reason people choose one.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are three types. A bypass humidifier uses the furnace blower to push some warm air through a water panel and back into the return duct; it is the simplest and cheapest, but only works when the blower runs. A fan-powered humidifier adds its own fan, so it does not need a bypass duct and typically delivers more moisture. A steam humidifier boils water with electrodes or a heating element and injects steam into the ductwork; it delivers the most output and works even when the furnace is not heating, but it costs the most to buy, install and run.
          </p>

          <p>
            Installed prices vary widely by region and by how much ductwork, plumbing and wiring is needed, but typical quotes run from several hundred dollars for a bypass or fan-powered unit to well over a thousand for steam. Running costs are mostly water, because bypass and fan-powered designs send some water down the drain to keep the panel flushed, plus electricity for fan or steam models. Our <a href="/whole-house-humidifier/">whole house humidifier guide</a> covers cost, installation and whether you need one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whole-house systems suit homes with forced-air heat in cold, dry climates, where every room dries out at once and portable units would mean several tanks to fill daily. They are not an option for homes heated by radiators, baseboards or ductless mini-splits; that is where a console unit earns its place. Specific models are ranked in our <a href="/best-whole-house-humidifier/">best whole house humidifier</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Which Type Fits Your Room
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with three questions. First, how hard is your water, and are you willing to buy distilled water or cartridges? Second, will the unit run in a bedroom where noise matters, or near children and pets where a hot reservoir is a hazard? Third, how much space are you humidifying, and does your home have forced-air ducts? Those answers eliminate most of the options before price comes into it.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Your situation</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bedroom, soft water, light sleeper</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ultrasonic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Near-silent and cheap to run</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bedroom, hard water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Evaporative or steam</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No white dust from tap water</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Child's room</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cool mist (evaporative or ultrasonic)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No hot water to spill</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Large living room or open plan</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Console evaporative</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High output, self-limiting</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whole house with forced-air heat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bypass or fan-powered central</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No tanks to fill</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whole house, very dry, high demand</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Steam central</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Highest output, works without heat call</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Desk or travel</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Personal ultrasonic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Small and portable</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Cost is the last filter rather than the first. A small ultrasonic unit can cost very little up front but more over time if you buy distilled water, while an evaporative unit costs more in wicks and a warm mist unit more in electricity. Our breakdown of <a href="/how-much-does-a-humidifier-cost/">how much a humidifier costs</a> runs the numbers for purchase price and a full heating season of consumables and power.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever type you choose, buy a hygrometer or choose a model with a built-in humidistat, and keep the reading between 30 and 50 percent. An oversized or always-on humidifier causes more problems than a dry room, including condensation on windows and mold in exterior walls. When you are ready to choose a model, our <a href="/best-humidifier/">best humidifier</a> guide ranks the strongest options in each type.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing by Form Factor Before Mechanism</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A sleek bedside unit and a big console can work in completely different ways. Decide on evaporative, ultrasonic or steam first, based on your water and your room, and only then pick a size. Choosing the shape first is how people end up with white dust or a loud fan they did not expect.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running an Ultrasonic Unit on Hard Tap Water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ultrasonic and impeller humidifiers spray dissolved minerals into the air, which settle as white dust on furniture and electronics. On hard water, either budget for distilled water or demineralization cartridges, or choose an evaporative or steam model that leaves minerals behind inside the unit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a Warm Mist Humidifier Within Reach of Children</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Steam and warm mist units hold water hot enough to scald if the unit tips over. Pediatric guidance commonly recommends cool mist humidifiers for children's rooms. If you want warm mist elsewhere in the house, place it on a stable, high surface out of reach of kids and pets.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the Coverage Figure on the Box</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Coverage ratings assume ideal conditions: standard ceilings, a closed room and a reasonably tight house. Open plans, high ceilings and very cold outdoor air all reduce real coverage. Compare rated output in gallons per day instead, and size up if your room is large or drafty.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating Filterless as Maintenance-Free</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                No wick means nothing to replace, but the tank and transducer still collect scale and grow bacteria if water sits. Every type needs a regular rinse, a weekly descale and a dry-out when stored. A filterless humidifier that is never cleaned can be worse for the air than no humidifier.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are the main types of humidifiers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There are four mechanisms: evaporative humidifiers that blow air through a wet wick, ultrasonic humidifiers that vibrate water into mist, impeller humidifiers that fling water at a diffuser, and steam humidifiers that boil water. Hybrids combine ultrasonic mist with a heater. These come in personal, tabletop, room and console sizes, plus whole-house systems mounted on furnace ductwork.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which type of humidifier is best?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on your water and room. Evaporative humidifiers are the safest all-rounder because they do not produce white dust and regulate themselves. Ultrasonic units are best for quiet bedrooms on soft water. Steam units give the cleanest output but use the most power. Whole-house units suit homes with forced-air heat in dry climates.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which humidifiers have filters?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Evaporative humidifiers use a wick or filter that soaks up water and traps minerals, and it needs replacing every one to three months, according to typical manufacturer guidance. Some ultrasonic models use optional demineralization cartridges. Steam and most ultrasonic units are filterless, though they still need regular descaling and cleaning.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do ultrasonic humidifiers cause white dust?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, when filled with hard tap water. The mist carries dissolved minerals, which settle as fine white dust after the droplets evaporate. The EPA recommends using low-mineral water in ultrasonic and impeller humidifiers. Distilled water or a demineralization cartridge largely solves it; evaporative and steam humidifiers avoid it by design.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a central humidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A central, or whole-house, humidifier mounts on the ductwork of a forced-air furnace and humidifies air as the heating system circulates it. It connects to a water supply and usually a drain, so there is no tank to refill. The three types are bypass, fan-powered and steam, in rising order of output and cost.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are portable humidifiers as good as whole-house ones?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For one or two rooms, a portable humidifier is cheaper and perfectly effective. For a whole house with forced-air heat in a cold climate, a central unit is far more convenient because it treats every room at once without tanks. Homes without ducts cannot use a central unit, so a console humidifier is the alternative.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which humidifier type uses the least electricity?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Ultrasonic and evaporative humidifiers typically draw only tens of watts, mainly for a small fan or the transducer. Steam and warm mist units commonly draw around 200 to 400 watts because they boil water. Running a 300-watt warm mist unit for ten hours a day uses about 3 kWh, roughly ten times an ultrasonic unit.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

